Project "tellme_local_state" {
  database_type: "JSON Lines (append-only local files)"
  Note: '''
  The persisted local-state model for tellme. Three artifacts share this model: (1) the per-mode
  session-history store (rounds 007–008, 014, 027) and (2) the shared global prompt log (round 015).
  The session-history store: one conversation is a sequence of completed turns, stored as append-only
  JSON Lines under the per-mode session workspace ($TELL_ME_HOME/output/<mode>/). The active file is
  `history.jsonl`; `--new` moves its entries into `history.archive.jsonl`. The store is read wholesale
  on resume and written one line per completed turn. Round 008 widens a completed turn to also carry the
  tool activity it performed (the agent tool loop). Round 014 adds an optional, provider-agnostic
  `signature` to each tool step (the opaque token the model emitted for that call — for example the
  Gemini `thoughtSignature`), persisted only when the provider supplies one and omitted when empty, so a
  resumed session replays the step faithfully. Round 027 adds `calls`: the turn's AI-endpoint-call count
  (provider inference rounds), summed across the active session to number the round-017 turn header. The record is
  `{prompt, answer, calls, steps:[{tool, arguments, result, signature?}]}`.
  The global prompt log (round 015; relocated round 028): the shared, append-only prompt log now at the
  USER home (~/.tellme/global_prompts.jsonl, resolved via os.UserHomeDir() — the same user-global root
  as the round-026 tool-usage log), shared across every environment/repository/mode, written only under
  `-i`, and read newest-first + deduplicated to seed interactive suggestions. When the file is absent it
  is seeded with a verbatim copy of the environment-scoped $TELL_ME_HOME/output/global_prompts.jsonl
  (copy, not move; never overwritten; a missing source starts empty); tellme no longer shares it with
  tell-me-go.
  The per-mode API-call usage log (round 018): one JSON record per provider call at
  $TELL_ME_HOME/output/<mode>/tokens.log, appended after every call in a turn and summed to give the
  session's token totals + cost for the post-turn status lines; `--new` archives/rotates it.
  The global tool-usage log (round 026): one JSON record per EXECUTED agent-tool invocation at
  ~/.tellme/tools-count.jsonl — a user-global, append-only log (shared across repos/envs/personas; the
  only artifact here that lives OUTSIDE $TELL_ME_HOME) recording {timestamp, tool, outcome}; it is never
  reset by `--new`.
  None of these artifacts is a relational database — this DBML pins the record shapes and lifecycles so
  the CLI contract, the interface feature truth, and the implementation share a single model.
  '''
}

Enum history_location {
  active
  archived
}

Enum tool_usage_outcome {
  ok
  error
  timeout
}

Table history_entry {
  location history_location
  position int
  prompt text
  answer text
  calls int

  indexes {
    (location, position) [pk]
  }

  Note: 'One completed exchange — the operator prompt and the provider answer — persisted as a single JSON line. Entries are append-only and ordered: within a file, `position` is the 1-based line ordinal (the record identity, since no ID or timestamp is stored) and the row order is the conversation order. `location` is `active` in `history.jsonl` and `archived` in `history.archive.jsonl`; `--new` moves every active entry to `archived` (entries from successive archived conversations append in order). An interrupted turn is never written, so no partial entry exists. Round 008: the entry ALSO embeds its ordered tool steps (see `history_step`), each optionally carrying a provider `signature` (round 014); `-l N` still surfaces only `prompt` and `answer`. Round 027 adds `calls` (int, always ≥ 1): the number of AI-endpoint calls — provider inference rounds — the turn made (1 for a tool-less turn, 1 + the number of tool rounds otherwise; an internal retry does not count). The value is summed across the ACTIVE session (`Σ calls`) and the round-017 turn header shows `Σ calls + 1`; a legacy line without the field counts as 1.'
}

Table history_step {
  location history_location
  position int
  step int
  tool varchar
  arguments text
  result text
  signature varchar

  indexes {
    (location, position, step) [pk]
  }

  Note: 'One tool execution performed during a completed turn (round 008 agent tool loop), embedded in the turn line as an element of its ordered steps array. `step` is the 1-based ordinal within the turn (which tool ran first, second, …); `tool` is the tool name (for example "list_files" or "read_files"); `arguments` is the model arguments (JSON-encoded) and `result` is the tool result or error, size-bounded (for example a file read capped at 1 MiB). Round 014 adds `signature`: the opaque, provider-agnostic token the provider emitted for this tool call (for example the Gemini 3 `thoughtSignature`), persisted only when the provider supplies one and omitted when empty (so a signature-less step serialises byte-identically to the round-008 shape). Read back and replayed VERBATIM into the conversation on resume, so a tool-using Gemini session replays the call faithfully; tellme never interprets, transforms, or expires it. Replayed as part of the synthesised tool call (no per-step id is stored — the adapter synthesises a deterministic tool-call id, `call_step_<step>`, when replaying); never surfaced by `-l`. A turn that made no tool calls has no `history_step` rows.'
}

Table prompt_log_entry {
  timestamp varchar
  prompt text

  indexes {
    (timestamp, prompt) [pk]
  }

  Note: 'One recorded operator prompt in the shared, append-only global prompt log (round 015) — one JSON object per line, `{"timestamp":"<RFC3339>","prompt":"<text>"}` (both fields string). The file lives at the USER home (`~/.tellme/global_prompts.jsonl`, resolved via `os.UserHomeDir()`) — the user-global `~/.tellme/` root (round 028), shared across every environment/repository/mode and NOT with tell-me-go (which keeps the environment-scoped `$TELL_ME_HOME/output/global_prompts.jsonl`). When the file is absent it is seeded with a VERBATIM copy of the environment-scoped `$TELL_ME_HOME/output/global_prompts.jsonl` (copy, not move; never overwrites an existing destination; a missing source starts empty). Written ONLY for an `-i` interactive submission; a non-`-i` run (positional/piped/non-interactive) reads and writes nothing. The write is append-only (`O_APPEND|O_CREATE`) and must never truncate or rewrite lines another writer added; no cross-process `flock` is taken. Reads scan backwards newest-first, deduplicating by `prompt` text and bounded by the interactive suggestion cap. A best-effort async compaction (performed inside the append lock, optimistic size-checked) rewrites the file to keep the newest ≤1200 unique prompts once it grows past ≈150 KiB. Distinct from the per-session `history_entry`/`history_step` store — this log is prompt-only and not session-scoped.'
}

Table usage_record {
  location history_location
  position int
  timestamp varchar
  provider varchar
  model varchar
  cached_tokens int
  prompt_tokens int
  response_tokens int
  thinking_tokens int
  total_tokens int
  cost decimal

  indexes {
    (location, position) [pk]
  }

  Note: 'One provider call''s recorded usage in the per-mode API-call usage log (round 018) — a single JSON object per line, `{"timestamp","provider","model","cached_tokens","prompt_tokens","response_tokens","total_tokens","thinking_tokens","cost"}`, appended to `$TELL_ME_HOME/output/<mode>/tokens.log` (the same per-mode workspace as `history.jsonl`). Append-only and ordered: `position` is the 1-based line ordinal (the record identity — no id is stored) and row order is call order. Written after EVERY provider call in a turn — the prompt completion and each tool-loop call — so the just-returned call is the last line. `cached_tokens` and `thinking_tokens` are the provider-reported cache-hit and reasoning counts (0 when the provider omits them); `prompt_tokens` INCLUDES the cached portion (so miss = prompt − cached); `total_tokens = prompt + response + thinking`; `cost` is the call cost computed from the config `MODELS` pricing table (0 when the model is un-priced). The session summary (`╰─⠿ Ready`, emitted on `stderr`) sums the whole file — session miss/cached/output token totals, session cost, and the cache-hit rate `cached/(miss+cached)`; the metrics line reports the last line. `--new` moves active records to `archived` (the archived file `tokens.archive.jsonl`, alongside `history.archive.jsonl`), so a fresh session starts with an empty active log. (Forward note: the per-mode directory now rotates two files — `history.jsonl` and `tokens.log` — with no cross-file atomicity and no `flock`; acceptable single-process, revisited if concurrent writers arrive.) Distinct from `history_entry`/`history_step` (the conversation) and `prompt_log_entry` (the shared prompt log). The log is a local audit/roll-up only — it never feeds the request payload. A companion `tokens.summary.json` in the same directory holds the incremental cumulative roll-up (session miss/hit/out + cost) the summary line reads in O(1) instead of re-parsing the log; `--new` drops it.'
}

Table tool_usage_record {
  position int [pk]
  timestamp varchar
  tool varchar
  outcome tool_usage_outcome

  Note: 'One agent-tool invocation''s recorded outcome in the user-global tool-usage log (round 026) — a single JSON object per line, `{"timestamp":"<RFC3339>","tool":"<wire name>","outcome":"<ok|error|timeout>"}`, appended to `~/.tellme/tools-count.jsonl` (the USER home, resolved via `os.UserHomeDir()` — deliberately OUTSIDE the runtime home: this log is global, shared across repos/envs/personas, unlike every other artifact here, which lives under `$TELL_ME_HOME`). One record per EXECUTED invocation (the tool name resolves in the registry and Execute runs): `outcome` is `ok` (a result — INCLUDING a bounded/truncated one), `error` (the tool returned a non-nil error), or `timeout` (a nil-error result at the per-call deadline — the round-024 FR-018 "stopped at the time limit" path). Append-only (`O_APPEND|O_CREATE`, 0o644); `position` is the 1-based line ordinal (the record identity — no id is stored) and row order is invocation order. Best-effort: an append failure, or an unresolvable user home, is a silent no-op that never breaks a turn. NEVER reset, archived, or truncated by `--new` (a fresh session must not zero the global counters) — unlike the per-mode `usage_record` log, which `--new` rotates. The `--tool-usage` offline report streams the file in ONE pass (O(tools) memory — never whole-file materialised) and sums per tool (every registered tool from the LIVE registry is shown, including zero-use ones, in registry order); it is RESILIENT — a malformed/torn line (a concurrent writer''s half-written final line, no `flock` being taken) is skipped best-effort and the report never fails or corrupts the roll-up on log content. The `~/.tellme/` directory and the file are created LAZILY on the first `Record` (a tool-less turn leaves `~/.tellme` untouched); `timestamp` is the adapter''s `time.Now()` (unasserted). Growth is unbounded (compaction is a forward item). Distinct from `history_step` (per-session conversation) and `usage_record` (per-mode provider-call tokens); the log never feeds the request payload.'
}

Table turns_log_line {
  position int [pk]
  text varchar

  Note: 'One line of the per-session turn log (round 053; ADR 0022) — the session''s RENDERED turn chrome, persisted VERBATIM as plain text (no JSON envelope) to `$TELL_ME_HOME/output/<mode>/turns.log` (the same per-mode workspace as `history.jsonl`/`tokens.log`). `position` is the 1-based line ordinal (the identity — no id/timestamp is stored); `text` is a single control-free line exactly as the diagnostic stream emitted it — the `─` turn rule, the `╭─⠿ Turn <N> - <mode>` header, the `[HH:MM:SS] Payload: …` status lines, and the `[HH:MM:SS] [<provider>] M: …` metrics line — so the file is a shadow of the chrome and invents NO new format (the round-017/018/022/040 chrome contracts are inherited verbatim; the round-039 terminal-safe-line policy means the lines carry no ANSI/control bytes). Written on the turn path by an injected store (bound at `cmd/tellme`), BEST-EFFORT — a write failure never fails the turn. Read by the `-t`/`--turns` offline command (prints the file to `stdout`, exit 0; a missing/empty file is success). `--new` moves active lines to `archived` (a sibling `turns.archive.log`), leaving a fresh session empty. Distinct from `history_entry` (the conversation), `usage_record` (per-call tokens), and `prompt_log_entry` (the shared prompt log); the log is a local trace only — it never feeds the request payload. RECORDED DIVERGENCE: this is tellme''s OWN chrome, not the reference''s `turns.log` bytes.'
}

Ref: history_step.(location, position) > history_entry.(location, position)
